# Ports-first

When you need a new external capability:

1. Define / extend a Protocol in `app/core/ports/<name>.py`. The Protocol is
   the contract; it owns the dataclasses (Bar, Quote, Order, Fill, …) used at
   the boundary.
2. Add a stub adapter in `app/connectors/<vendor>_client.py` (3rd-party SaaS)
   or `app/services/<area>/<vendor>.py` (local service). Adapter implements
   only the Protocol — no extra public methods.
3. Wire the adapter into `app/core/di/container.py:Container.build`.
4. Domain code (`app/core/ai/**`, `app/core/ai/risk/**`,
   `app/core/ai/scenarios/**`) imports the **Protocol**, never the adapter.

## Why

Lets us swap Polygon ↔ Alpaca ↔ ParquetMarketData in backtest without touching
domain code. Also forces us to model what we actually need from a vendor before
writing code that depends on a vendor's quirks.

## Smells to reject

- A `from polygon import …` inside `app/core/`
- A new method on `PolygonMarketData` that isn't on `MarketDataProvider`
- A direct `redis.asyncio` call in a feature/model module (use `KVCache`)
- Any `if isinstance(broker, AlpacaBroker)` in domain code
